      "output": "**Water** is a simple chemical compound that is essential for life on Earth. Its basic characteristics are:\n\n| Aspect | Details |\n|--------|---------|\n| **Chemical formula** | **H₂O** – two hydrogen atoms covalently bonded to one oxygen atom. |\n| **Molecular structure** | Bent (angular) shape with a bond angle of about 104.5°, giving the molecule a permanent dipole (partial negative charge on oxygen, partial positive on hydrogens). |\n| **Physical states** | Exists naturally as a solid (ice), liquid (water), and gas (water vapor/steam) depending on temperature and pressure. |\n| **Key physical properties** | • **Boiling point:** 100 °C (212 °F) at 1 atm<br>• **Freezing point:** 0 °C (32 °F) at 1 atm<br>• **Density:** ~1 g cm⁻³ (liquid at 4 °C)<br>• **High specific heat:** 4.18 J g⁻¹ K⁻¹ (helps moderate climate).<br>• **Surface tension:** ~72 mN m⁻¹ at 20 °C (allows capillary action). |\n| **Polarity & hydrogen bonding** | The dipole moment enables **hydrogen bonds** between neighboring molecules. These bonds are weaker than covalent bonds but strong enough to give water its high boiling point, surface tension, and ability to dissolve many substances. |\n| **Solvent properties** | Often called the “universal solvent” because it can dissolve ionic compounds (e.g., salts) and polar molecules (e.g., sugars, alcohols). This makes it crucial for biochemical reactions and transport of nutrients in living organisms. |\n| **Biological importance** | • Constitutes ~60 % of the human body by weight.<br>• Acts as a medium for metabolic reactions, temperature regulation, and waste removal.<br>• Provides habitat for aquatic ecosystems. |\n| **Environmental role** | • Drives the **hydrologic (water) cycle**: evaporation → condensation → precipitation → runoff → infiltration.<br>• Regulates Earth’s climate through heat storage and transport"
